 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "The staff party is under way. Max and Kostya have both bought rings, Senya can't resist teasing, and then Max is pulled into a drinking contest. Here's the Russian.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ну что, купил кольцо?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Да. А ты?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я тоже.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Покажи.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Вы друг другу предложение делаете, да?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А тебе что?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ничего. Ребята, когда свадьба?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Что, подарил?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Ещё не успел. Сейчас пойду.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Ну, я пошёл.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Удачи!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "И сегодня против Саши — Макс Лавров!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Не, ребят, я пас.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ты что, боишься?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Наливай. Поехали.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now each line in turn.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ну что, купил кольцо?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'So, did you buy the ring?' In a quick question Russian often drops ty, 'you', and just says kupil koltso, 'bought the ring?'",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Да. А ты?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes. And you?' The little word a, 'and', turns the question back, like English 'and you?'",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я тоже.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Me too.' Russian says ya tozhe, literally 'I also', where English says 'me too'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Покажи.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Show me.' The word pokazhi, 'show', is the friendly command, and the 'me' is understood.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Вы друг другу предложение делаете, да?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You're proposing to each other, right?' Russian says delat predlozhenie, literally 'make an offer', for proposing marriage.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А тебе что?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What's it to you?' Russian says a tebe chto, literally 'and to you what', to mean 'what's it to you?'",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ничего. Ребята, когда свадьба?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Nothing. Guys, when's the wedding?' The word rebyata, 'guys', is the friendly way to call to a group of people.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Что, подарил?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'So, did you give it to her?' The word podaril means 'gave as a present', and comes from podarok, 'present'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Ещё не успел. Сейчас пойду.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I haven't had the chance yet. I'll go now.' The word uspel means 'managed in time', so ne uspel is 'didn't get round to it'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Ну, я пошёл.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, I'm off.' Russian says ya poshyol, literally 'I went', to mean 'I'm leaving right now'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Удачи!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good luck!' The word udachi literally means 'of luck', short for 'I wish you luck'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "И сегодня против Саши — Макс Лавров!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And today, against Sasha, it's Max Lavrov!' After protiv, 'against', a name takes a new ending, so Sasha ends in -i here.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Не, ребят, я пас.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'No, guys, I'm out.' Russian says ya pas, 'I pass', a word borrowed from card games.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ты что, боишься?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What, are you scared?' Russian says ty chto, literally 'you what', to mean 'what's wrong with you?'",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Наливай. Поехали.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Pour it. Let's go.' The word poekhali literally means 'we drove off', and Russians say it for 'here we go'. Here's the party scene again.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Ну что, купил кольцо?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "kostya",
   "t": "Да. А ты?",
   "gap": 0.5
